A null pointer exception is a runtime exception that occurs when you try to access or manipulate a null (non-existent) object in your code.

A null pointer is an object that has no value or no reference to any object. It's essentially an empty object.

Here's an example in Java:

```java
public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String str = null;
        System.out.println(str.length()); // This will throw a NullPointerException
    }
}
```

In this example, `str` is a null reference because it's not assigned any value.

When you try to access `str.length()` in the `main` method, it throws a `NullPointerException`.

To avoid this, you should always check if a variable is null before trying to access its properties or methods. Here's how you can do it in Java:

```java
public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String str = null;
        if (str != null) {
            System.out.println(str.length()); // This will not throw an exception
        } else {
            System.out.println("String is null");
        }
    }
}
```

In this example, the `if` statement checks if `str` is not null before trying to access its properties. If `str` is null, the `else` block is executed.

Remember, null pointer exceptions can be very useful in certain situations, such as when you're working with database connections or file handles, and you don't want to access those resources until they're actually available.

